<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chinon is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, dominated by a large medieval castle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, who died there in 1189, used it as a favourite residence and treasury, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> took it in 1205. In 1429 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> met the future Charles VII at Chinon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinon.jpg" alt="Chinon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinon_-_View_from_the_castle_-_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
